<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/advanced/bayesian-statistics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Bayesian statistics treats probability as <strong>degrees of belief that update with
    evidence</strong> — formally, via Bayes' theorem. Where classical testing asks "how
    surprising is this data if the null is true?", the Bayesian asks directly: "given this data,
    how believable is each hypothesis?" The price of that directness: you must state a
    <strong>prior</strong> — and defend it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the theorem, the update loop, and the medical-test trap that saves lives to understand" widget="equationStep" config={bayesCfg} />

  <h2>Why the approach took over</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It answers the question you actually asked.</strong> P(hypothesis | data) reads forward; classical p-values answer the awkward inverse P(data | hypothesis).</li>
    <li><strong>Priors are not cheating.</strong> They encode real knowledge (previous studies, physics constraints) and their influence shrinks as data accumulates. Sensitivity analysis shows when priors matter.</li>
    <li><strong>Posterior distributions are richer than point estimates</strong> — full uncertainty over every parameter, sampled by modern methods (MCMC; Stan, PyMC).</li>
    <li><strong>The frequentist–Bayesian split</strong> is philosophical (long-run behaviour vs belief updating), but modern practice is pragmatic: use whichever answers the question, and know both.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bayes (1763); McElreath, Statistical Rethinking; Gelman et al., Bayesian Data Analysis.</p>
</TopicPage>
